INT. MONASTERY BELL TOWER - DAWN

Narrow stone stair, spiraling up into dark. At the top: a square chamber, open arches on four sides, a great bronze bell hanging dead still. Fog beyond the arches. Below, somewhere, artillery coughs.

SERGEANT IGNATIUS OBUYA, 30s, mud to the knees, rifle slung, wedges himself behind a pillar.

Behind him, PRIVATE TOBIAS WREN, 19, shaking hands, a field radio strapped to his back.

A STRANGER crouches in the corner. Gaunt. Brown habit, rope belt, bare feet. He has been there all night.

OBUYA
(low)
Who's that?

WREN
Monk, I think.

OBUYA
(to the Stranger)
You speak English?

The Stranger nods, once.

STRANGER
Some. Brother Anselm.

OBUYA
Brother, you can't be up here.

ANSELM
I have rung this bell every dawn for thirty years.

Another shell. The tower shudders. Dust falls.

WREN
Sarge, the ridge is full of them.

OBUYA
Count.

WREN
Forty. Fifty.

OBUYA
Radio. Tell battalion we're in position.

Wren fumbles with the handset. Static. Nothing.

WREN
Dead.

OBUYA
Then we hold.

Anselm stands. He looks at Obuya. Points to the bell, then to himself.

ANSELM
They will hear it. They always stop.

OBUYA
Brother, if you ring that, they'll know exactly where we are.

ANSELM
They know already.

He lifts the rope and tugs once.

A single, enormous BONG.

OBUYA
No!

Anselm rings it again. And again. The tower hums with it. Below, in the fog, the guns fall silent.

Wren stares through the arch.

WREN
They're stopping.

OBUYA
What?

WREN
They're taking their hats off.

Obuya lowers his rifle. Anselm keeps ringing. Behind him, the sun breaks through the fog like a struck match.
